Troubleshooting Tips
Permission Denied Errors:

Ensure you run the script with sudo for log collection
Check that you have write permissions in the evidence directory
Missing Commands:

If netstat is not found, use ss -tuln instead
Install missing packages: sudo apt install net-tools
Empty Log Files:

Some systems use journalctl instead of traditional logs
Alternative: sudo journalctl > $EVIDENCE_DIR/logs/journal.log
Hash Verification Failures:

Ensure you don't modify files after hash creation
Re-run hash creation if files were changed
Conclusion
You have successfully created an automated evidence collection script that:

Gathers critical system information including hostname, OS details, and kernel version
Collects user activity and process information for analysis
Captures network configuration and active connections
Preserves system logs with proper timestamps
Generates cryptographic hashes (MD5 and SHA256) for evidence integrity
Creates secure, read-only archives of collected evidence
Real-World Significance: This type of automated collection is essential in incident response and forensic investigations. It ensures consistent, repeatable evidence gathering while maintaining chain of custody. The hash values provide cryptographic proof that evidence hasn't been tampered with, which is critical for legal proceedings.

